WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.009 --> 00:00:05.610
I'm here not to tell you that Canonical is pivoting to becoming an AI company,

2
00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:07.490
but to tell you that it's been here all along.

3
00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:11.050
Ubuntu powers the majority of today's AI workloads.

4
00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:24.760
Unfortunately, I think I am going to contribute to the AI native dev eye test

5
00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:27.440
because I have dark slides and light slides, so apologies for that.

6
00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:30.920
As Alan said, I'm Jon. I'm the VP of Engineering for Ubuntu.

7
00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:33.060
I've been at Canonical for five years.

8
00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:37.620
In that time, I've mostly worked on cloud native orchestration-type tools,

9
00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:40.520
but in the last year, I've been working on this little open-source project

10
00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:41.840
that some of you might have heard of called Ubuntu.

11
00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:48.740
I'm here not to tell you that Canonical is pivoting to becoming an AI company.

12
00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:51.100
I don't think that's the case.

13
00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:52.940
It's certainly not in my immediate roadmap,

14
00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:55.660
but to tell you that it's been here all along, in a sense.

15
00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:00.240
Ubuntu has been here for the last 21 years, 22 years,

16
00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:02.780
and I suspect it will be here for the next 20 years

17
00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:06.100
if we don't make any huge mistakes in the coming probably three to four years.

18
00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:10.140
We are in the privileged position where I can say this today,

19
00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:14.800
which is that Ubuntu powers the majority of today's AI workloads.

20
00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:18.400
You might ask, why is that?

21
00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:21.480
Some of it is because the wonderful agents that we have today,

22
00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:24.040
when you ask them how you do something, are really good at saying,

23
00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:28.220
on Ubuntu, sometimes on Ubuntu or Debian, type this command.

24
00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:31.980
And I think that is a representation of the fact that for 20 years,

25
00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:34.100
the orange Linux has been the one that people reach for.

26
00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:37.380
That is a combination of exceptional strategy

27
00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:41.020
from the previous people at Canonical and a healthy dollop of luck.

28
00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:43.620
But when you launch cloud instances today,

29
00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:47.400
most of you probably don't care what kind of Linux you get,

30
00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:49.260
but it's probably our one in reality.

31
00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:51.240
It doesn't matter whether you're on Google Cloud,

32
00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:52.960
whether you're on Amazon, whether you're on DigitalOcean,

33
00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:55.340
or Volta, or Hetzner, or whatever you like.

34
00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:58.920
If you launch a VM, there's a good chance it's going to be Ubuntu.

35
00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:01.760
Whether or not we are, in fact,

36
00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:03.480
at the hallowed year of the Linux desktop,

37
00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:06.100
we have very much been year of the Linux server

38
00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:09.020
for many, many years.

39
00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:12.160
But none of those things are particularly new.

40
00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:14.040
Those have been facts for some time.

41
00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:16.360
It's long before I got involved with Ubuntu.

42
00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:20.500
One of the things that I think is really fascinating

43
00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:22.220
in this particular era,

44
00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:23.980
is something that no one really talks about,

45
00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:26.700
because it's not glamorous, it's not glitzy,

46
00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:30.360
it doesn't get great headlines on Reddit and Phoronix,

47
00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:32.320
where they like to tell us that our choices are wrong

48
00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:34.140
for choosing Rust and such.

49
00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:38.520
But it's actually our very, very strong partner business.

50
00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:40.180
So we're actually a pretty small company

51
00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:41.300
in the enterprise Linux game.

52
00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:43.640
We're about 1,300 people,

53
00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:46.060
of which about 1,000 are engineers.

54
00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:49.820
When I joined five years ago, we were 550 in total.

55
00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:52.880
But the fact remains that SUSE is,

56
00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:54.220
I think, five times our size,

57
00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:55.820
and Red Hat is 25 times our size,

58
00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:58.700
if you ignore the big IBM-shaped thing on the side of it.

59
00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:01.420
So we are relatively little,

60
00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:03.660
but I think we punch well above our weight

61
00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:05.620
when it comes to working with the people you see on screen,

62
00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:06.200
and more.

63
00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:07.860
So I couldn't get all of the logos on here,

64
00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:08.780
but there's lots of fun ones,

65
00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:11.420
like MediaTek and RivOS, and all these things.

66
00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:14.220
And this makes a huge difference,

67
00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:15.620
because this means when you go down

68
00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:18.600
to whichever consumer electronics store you want,

69
00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:20.900
or whichever internet business you buy your computer from,

70
00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:22.580
and you want to play with AI stuff,

71
00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:23.720
you want to get working with it,

72
00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:26.620
you open the lid, and it is actually going to work.

73
00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:29.720
This is particularly true of NVIDIA hardware

74
00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:31.060
in recent months,

75
00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:33.160
but we are expanding massively with AMD.

76
00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:35.380
We have always done lots of work with Intel.

77
00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:37.760
And with things like Qualcomm Dragonwing,

78
00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:39.940
which is one of their kind of edge IoT platforms,

79
00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:41.900
we were right there on launch day, good to go,

80
00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:43.060
with support in the kernel,

81
00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:44.880
support for all of the accelerators, right?

82
00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:46.940
Not just the CPU and GPU,

83
00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:49.200
but all of the NPUs and TPUs,

84
00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:52.780
and sort of star PUs that they're starting to introduce.

85
00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:56.200
And this, I think, was a pretty big moment.

86
00:03:56.620 --> 00:03:59.640
So there's this little Silicon company called NVIDIA

87
00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:00.600
that some of you will have heard of,

88
00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:05.180
and they released an ARM64-only AI workstation

89
00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:06.580
called the NVIDIA DGX Spark,

90
00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:10.200
to much applause in the late part of last year.

91
00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:12.020
And what was different about this for us

92
00:04:12.020 --> 00:04:13.820
is NVIDIA have shipped Ubuntu for years,

93
00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:16.540
and they've done that in agreement with us,

94
00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:18.160
and it's been called DGX OS,

95
00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:21.800
or it's been called NVIDIA something OS,

96
00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:23.880
but it's been Ubuntu with a bunch of stuff installed

97
00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:25.280
and some different kernel configurations.

98
00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:27.860
And what changed for this is they just ship Ubuntu

99
00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:28.660
and they call it Ubuntu.

100
00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:31.280
And it is not only do they ship Ubuntu and call it Ubuntu,

101
00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:33.900
it is the only thing they will support on the DGX Spark.

102
00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:37.200
Now, I don't have all of their product roadmap,

103
00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:38.200
and I don't know what's coming up,

104
00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:39.660
but I'd take a pretty good educated guess

105
00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:42.640
that in the future, their other workstations,

106
00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:45.380
so this is the Grace Blackwell architecture,

107
00:04:45.380 --> 00:04:48.560
also seen sold by Dell, and I think Lenovo,

108
00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:50.660
and possibly HP, all of them run Ubuntu.

109
00:04:51.140 --> 00:04:52.100
And the reason they run Ubuntu

110
00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:53.740
is because they can boot Ubuntu on it,

111
00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:54.800
and it just works.

112
00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:56.280
Like all of the drivers are there,

113
00:04:56.400 --> 00:04:57.460
the kernel behaves properly,

114
00:04:57.580 --> 00:04:58.740
all of the accelerators are there.

115
00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:01.820
So this was a pretty huge moment for us,

116
00:05:01.900 --> 00:05:03.720
but I also think it was quite a big moment

117
00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:06.280
for the AI development community,

118
00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:09.280
because working in development on the operating system

119
00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:10.320
that is running in your cloud,

120
00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:11.660
I think is fairly obviously

121
00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:13.520
and has always been fairly obviously

122
00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:14.640
a bit of an advantage.

123
00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:18.560
This is something I'm very excited about.

124
00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:20.580
So we do a two-year LTS release.

125
00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:21.800
So these are the ones

126
00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:23.160
that realistically everybody runs,

127
00:05:23.380 --> 00:05:25.740
something like the late 90%

128
00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:27.900
of all of our users run an LTS,

129
00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:30.280
and they are 22.04, 24.04,

130
00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:32.420
and the one coming out in April is 26.04.

131
00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:34.040
And for the first time,

132
00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:35.720
you will be able to apt install CUDA

133
00:05:35.720 --> 00:05:37.380
or apt install ROCm

134
00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:40.100
with no other commands on a base Ubuntu system

135
00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:42.240
and get exactly the version of ROCm

136
00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:43.520
that works with your version of Ubuntu

137
00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:45.200
and your GPU with no messing about.

138
00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:47.600
And having personally suffered through this

139
00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:51.340
with a collection of quite high-end AMD machines

140
00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:52.600
and various versions of ROCm

141
00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:54.000
and the driver and various other things,

142
00:05:54.500 --> 00:05:57.260
and I'm told it is equally as painful in CUDA land,

143
00:05:57.740 --> 00:05:59.260
I think this is kind of a big deal.

144
00:05:59.540 --> 00:06:01.380
And it also speaks to that cloud story as well.

145
00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:02.880
In reality, all of the clouds

146
00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:04.960
are also running these big shiny green GPUs

147
00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:05.740
in their data centres

148
00:06:05.740 --> 00:06:07.680
and not investing engineering time

149
00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:08.920
in trying to work out

150
00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:10.340
how to get the latest version of CUDA

151
00:06:10.340 --> 00:06:12.480
security-maintained for 15 years,

152
00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:13.840
which is our kind of promise,

153
00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:15.800
I think is a pretty big thing.

154
00:06:15.940 --> 00:06:17.600
And it's particularly big

155
00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:20.080
for developers getting started in this industry.

156
00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:21.580
Lots of you will already know

157
00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:23.200
how to wrangle CUDA into shape

158
00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:24.920
and make ROCm work with PyTorch.

159
00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:25.580
Good for you.

160
00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:27.060
If you have any ideas,

161
00:06:27.140 --> 00:06:27.920
do please let me know.

162
00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:30.380
But I think it's going to get a lot easier

163
00:06:30.380 --> 00:06:31.180
because of this.

164
00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:33.020
It's just going to be out of the box, essentially.

165
00:06:35.780 --> 00:06:37.260
Having said that we're not an AI company,

166
00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:38.760
because I don't think we are, in quotes,

167
00:06:38.860 --> 00:06:39.700
an AI company,

168
00:06:39.700 --> 00:06:42.820
we have released something relatively new

169
00:06:42.820 --> 00:06:44.900
which is distinctly kind of an AI product.

170
00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:46.020
Not a product in the sense

171
00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:47.020
that we are monetising it

172
00:06:47.020 --> 00:06:47.820
and selling it to you.

173
00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:48.700
A product in the sense

174
00:06:48.700 --> 00:06:49.720
that you can go and get it

175
00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:50.820
and use it, it's open source

176
00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:51.560
and we built it.

177
00:06:52.340 --> 00:06:53.740
I think this is really interesting

178
00:06:53.740 --> 00:06:56.020
for the hobbyist, the tinkerer,

179
00:06:56.460 --> 00:06:58.060
the developer at the moment.

180
00:06:58.280 --> 00:06:59.860
But I think it has huge potential

181
00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:01.260
to go further than that.

182
00:07:01.660 --> 00:07:03.680
So in the world of Linux packaging

183
00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:06.600
and the microcosm of internet culture

184
00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:07.280
that that is,

185
00:07:07.720 --> 00:07:08.980
there are these things called snaps.

186
00:07:08.980 --> 00:07:10.240
Most people in the world

187
00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:11.580
don't care that there is a thing called snaps.

188
00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:12.680
It is a packaging format.

189
00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:14.300
It is a packaging format we invented

190
00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:16.200
and it has some interesting properties

191
00:07:17.220 --> 00:07:18.940
in many, many applications

192
00:07:18.940 --> 00:07:20.400
on the internet and in computing.

193
00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:22.160
But those exact same properties

194
00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:23.520
make it especially interesting,

195
00:07:23.620 --> 00:07:24.580
I think, in the world of AI.

196
00:07:24.980 --> 00:07:27.040
So snaps are a confined package format.

197
00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:29.440
Unlike a deb where you apt instal

198
00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:32.620
or an RPM package where you dnf instal

199
00:07:32.620 --> 00:07:33.520
or whatever it is,

200
00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:35.740
a snap is a little bit more

201
00:07:35.740 --> 00:07:36.520
like a docker container.

202
00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:38.700
It is a compressed file system

203
00:07:38.700 --> 00:07:39.660
full of an application

204
00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:40.720
and all of its dependencies.

205
00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:41.940
But critically,

206
00:07:42.220 --> 00:07:44.060
it runs in a security-confined environment.

207
00:07:44.300 --> 00:07:47.140
So we use the AppArmor Linux security module

208
00:07:47.140 --> 00:07:48.440
which is a bit like SE Linux

209
00:07:48.440 --> 00:07:49.040
for those of you

210
00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:50.060
that have been in the Linux space.

211
00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:51.780
And that means that you can instal a snap

212
00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:53.340
with all kinds of scary stuff in it

213
00:07:53.340 --> 00:07:55.460
and run it and not worry about it

214
00:07:55.460 --> 00:07:57.200
doing wild things to your machine.

215
00:07:57.420 --> 00:07:59.600
Does this sound familiar in the AI space?

216
00:08:01.100 --> 00:08:03.440
So we started distributing these things

217
00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:04.280
called inference snaps.

218
00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:06.240
And this is a bit of a play

219
00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:08.500
towards how do we make this stuff easy

220
00:08:08.500 --> 00:08:10.420
for people to get working with.

221
00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:11.160
At the moment,

222
00:08:11.260 --> 00:08:11.880
it's all very fun

223
00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:12.920
to be at the very cutting edge

224
00:08:12.920 --> 00:08:14.020
playing on Hugging Face

225
00:08:14.020 --> 00:08:15.080
and looking at the rankings

226
00:08:15.080 --> 00:08:15.760
and trying to work out

227
00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:17.140
which model is going to fit on your GPU.

228
00:08:17.720 --> 00:08:19.020
And do I want Lama CPP

229
00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:20.440
or do I not want Lama CPP?

230
00:08:21.880 --> 00:08:23.680
The 90% AI engineer,

231
00:08:23.980 --> 00:08:25.360
if not now in the next six months,

232
00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:25.900
is going to be like,

233
00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:26.680
give me the model.

234
00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:28.420
I've heard of Gemma.

235
00:08:28.540 --> 00:08:29.880
I've heard of Nemetron.

236
00:08:29.980 --> 00:08:30.660
I've heard of Quen.

237
00:08:30.760 --> 00:08:31.620
How do I play with it

238
00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:33.820
without getting a PhD in Hugging Face?

239
00:08:35.240 --> 00:08:36.299
So that's what we've done.

240
00:08:36.299 --> 00:08:38.539
Inference snaps are high quality

241
00:08:38.539 --> 00:08:40.940
silicon-optimised AI models for everybody.

242
00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:42.299
And this goes a bit back

243
00:08:42.299 --> 00:08:43.200
to my previous statement.

244
00:08:43.640 --> 00:08:44.400
The key here

245
00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:45.760
is they are actually optimised

246
00:08:45.760 --> 00:08:46.620
by the silicon company.

247
00:08:47.580 --> 00:08:50.060
So we are partnering with AMD

248
00:08:50.060 --> 00:08:51.660
and NVIDIA and Intel and whoever.

249
00:08:52.100 --> 00:08:53.700
When we first started talking about this,

250
00:08:53.740 --> 00:08:54.440
no one was really interested

251
00:08:54.440 --> 00:08:55.380
and when we launched the first one,

252
00:08:55.440 --> 00:08:56.480
everyone started kicking our door down.

253
00:08:56.620 --> 00:08:59.300
So we are slowly rolling out more models.

254
00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:01.140
But the gist of it is

255
00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:03.420
you can, on any Ubuntu machine in the world,

256
00:09:03.560 --> 00:09:05.180
type snap instal Quen VL,

257
00:09:05.180 --> 00:09:06.800
snap instal DeepSeq R1,

258
00:09:07.260 --> 00:09:08.200
snap instal Gemma 3,

259
00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:09.080
Nemetron 3 Nano,

260
00:09:09.540 --> 00:09:10.360
and what you will get

261
00:09:10.360 --> 00:09:11.520
is a working model

262
00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:12.640
that has been optimised

263
00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:14.080
for the hardware on your machine

264
00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:15.780
by the people who built the silicon,

265
00:09:16.340 --> 00:09:17.920
delivered by us and maintained by us.

266
00:09:19.180 --> 00:09:20.480
And it doesn't just come

267
00:09:20.480 --> 00:09:21.220
with the model itself

268
00:09:21.220 --> 00:09:22.360
because, again, what do you do

269
00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:23.500
if you just download the model

270
00:09:23.500 --> 00:09:24.240
in its bare form?

271
00:09:24.460 --> 00:09:25.540
Not super interesting.

272
00:09:26.060 --> 00:09:28.660
It comes in a silicon-optimised form

273
00:09:28.660 --> 00:09:30.660
with the right inference engine

274
00:09:30.660 --> 00:09:31.520
for that model

275
00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:32.680
according to the manufacturer.

276
00:09:33.140 --> 00:09:34.240
And it gives you

277
00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:35.260
this super nice onboarding

278
00:09:35.260 --> 00:09:36.100
if you just want to play

279
00:09:36.100 --> 00:09:37.800
with local AI models.

280
00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:39.480
You want to hook them up to Olama.

281
00:09:39.660 --> 00:09:40.200
You want to hook them up

282
00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:40.860
to, like, Continue,

283
00:09:41.340 --> 00:09:42.320
whatever it is you might want to do.

284
00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:44.920
This is the kind of, like,

285
00:09:44.940 --> 00:09:46.160
gory guts of it diagram.

286
00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:48.300
There's a lot going on here,

287
00:09:48.320 --> 00:09:49.600
but it's actually relatively simple.

288
00:09:49.780 --> 00:09:51.680
So the snap, the big orange box,

289
00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:53.840
is the SquashFS file system

290
00:09:53.840 --> 00:09:56.020
inside which is stuff, right?

291
00:09:56.120 --> 00:09:56.800
And we have this thing

292
00:09:56.800 --> 00:09:57.640
called an engine manager

293
00:09:57.640 --> 00:09:58.220
which is shared

294
00:09:58.220 --> 00:09:59.500
between all of our inference snaps.

295
00:09:59.700 --> 00:10:00.300
The engine manager

296
00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:01.320
has some nice capabilities

297
00:10:01.320 --> 00:10:03.240
like understanding what a machine is,

298
00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:05.980
what API level your card supports,

299
00:10:06.040 --> 00:10:06.660
that kind of thing.

300
00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:08.640
We have engine manifests

301
00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:10.520
which describe the different kinds

302
00:10:10.520 --> 00:10:11.260
of things you might be,

303
00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:12.660
like you're an AMD GPU,

304
00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:13.980
you're an NVIDIA-capable,

305
00:10:14.300 --> 00:10:15.980
sorry, a CUDA-capable NVIDIA GPU.

306
00:10:16.460 --> 00:10:17.580
And then we have the inference engine

307
00:10:17.580 --> 00:10:18.920
which is kind of swappable,

308
00:10:19.340 --> 00:10:20.220
could be Lama CPP,

309
00:10:20.440 --> 00:10:21.220
could be something else,

310
00:10:21.400 --> 00:10:22.480
and the runtime of the model.

311
00:10:23.240 --> 00:10:24.540
And all of that is hooked up

312
00:10:24.540 --> 00:10:25.380
kind of automatically

313
00:10:25.380 --> 00:10:26.240
when you instal it

314
00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:27.820
through the snap confinement

315
00:10:27.820 --> 00:10:28.660
so it kind of punches

316
00:10:28.660 --> 00:10:30.100
just enough hole

317
00:10:30.100 --> 00:10:31.220
through the confinement

318
00:10:31.220 --> 00:10:31.980
to speak to the kernel

319
00:10:31.980 --> 00:10:32.620
and get what it needs

320
00:10:32.620 --> 00:10:33.180
about the machine.

321
00:10:34.280 --> 00:10:35.160
But the outcome is this.

322
00:10:35.780 --> 00:10:37.460
You can snap instal QuenVL

323
00:10:37.460 --> 00:10:38.940
and then you can QuenVL chat.

324
00:10:39.340 --> 00:10:40.020
And in your terminal,

325
00:10:40.300 --> 00:10:41.160
wouldn't be a Linux talk

326
00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:42.120
without some terminal chat,

327
00:10:42.460 --> 00:10:43.140
but in your terminal

328
00:10:43.140 --> 00:10:44.260
you can chat with the model.

329
00:10:46.440 --> 00:10:47.360
We've got four of these

330
00:10:47.360 --> 00:10:47.860
at the moment.

331
00:10:48.620 --> 00:10:50.440
This is the least interesting thing

332
00:10:50.440 --> 00:10:51.200
you could do with it.

333
00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:51.900
It is also,

334
00:10:52.020 --> 00:10:52.800
every single one of them

335
00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:54.640
comes with an open AI

336
00:10:55.380 --> 00:10:57.260
API spec compatible endpoint, right?

337
00:10:57.320 --> 00:10:58.180
Just running on local host

338
00:10:58.180 --> 00:10:58.680
on a port.

339
00:10:58.800 --> 00:10:59.420
Each of them comes

340
00:10:59.420 --> 00:11:00.260
on a distinct port

341
00:11:00.260 --> 00:11:01.400
so you can co-instal them.

342
00:11:01.820 --> 00:11:02.560
Some of them can be running

343
00:11:02.560 --> 00:11:03.380
one engine and runtime.

344
00:11:03.560 --> 00:11:04.500
Some of them can be running another.

345
00:11:04.940 --> 00:11:05.440
You could have,

346
00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:07.020
if you've got a big honking machine

347
00:11:07.020 --> 00:11:08.060
with a bunch of GPUs in it,

348
00:11:08.120 --> 00:11:08.840
you can have one running

349
00:11:08.840 --> 00:11:10.760
on your ROCm capable AMD card.

350
00:11:10.860 --> 00:11:11.560
You could have one running

351
00:11:11.560 --> 00:11:14.080
on your CUDA capable NVIDIA card.

352
00:11:14.180 --> 00:11:14.820
That's not a problem.

353
00:11:15.200 --> 00:11:16.000
And basically anything

354
00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:17.700
that can speak the open AI

355
00:11:18.780 --> 00:11:19.860
API spec will be able

356
00:11:19.860 --> 00:11:20.540
to speak to this thing.

357
00:11:21.940 --> 00:11:22.540
All of the,

358
00:11:22.820 --> 00:11:23.620
I'll do a little demo

359
00:11:23.620 --> 00:11:24.160
of this at the end.

360
00:11:24.200 --> 00:11:25.200
All of the examples on here

361
00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:26.160
are talking to local host,

362
00:11:26.220 --> 00:11:26.940
but there is nothing

363
00:11:26.940 --> 00:11:28.520
to stop you slapping a caddy proxy

364
00:11:28.520 --> 00:11:29.160
in front of this

365
00:11:29.160 --> 00:11:30.520
and sticking it on a cloud machine

366
00:11:30.520 --> 00:11:31.960
with a big honking H100 in it

367
00:11:31.960 --> 00:11:32.720
or something like that

368
00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:33.820
and using it.

369
00:11:34.280 --> 00:11:34.780
So this,

370
00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:36.780
assuming you have access

371
00:11:36.780 --> 00:11:37.480
to cloud machinery

372
00:11:37.480 --> 00:11:38.540
that has access to Ubuntu,

373
00:11:38.820 --> 00:11:39.800
which is I think everybody,

374
00:11:40.260 --> 00:11:41.140
then you can make use

375
00:11:41.140 --> 00:11:41.780
of this immediately.

376
00:11:41.940 --> 00:11:42.720
But it's particularly fun

377
00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:43.520
to play with on a machine

378
00:11:43.520 --> 00:11:44.740
that has some capability.

379
00:11:45.100 --> 00:11:45.560
Unfortunately,

380
00:11:45.560 --> 00:11:46.180
when I do the demo,

381
00:11:46.260 --> 00:11:46.740
I'm talking to you

382
00:11:46.740 --> 00:11:47.240
on a thing that has

383
00:11:47.240 --> 00:11:48.140
absolutely no acceleration

384
00:11:48.140 --> 00:11:49.480
whatsoever, so it's real slow.

385
00:11:49.680 --> 00:11:50.400
But I promise you,

386
00:11:50.420 --> 00:11:51.060
it's cool on a machine

387
00:11:51.060 --> 00:11:51.780
that's a little bit quicker

388
00:11:51.780 --> 00:11:52.320
than my laptop.

389
00:11:54.860 --> 00:11:55.880
The other thing

390
00:11:56.380 --> 00:11:57.320
that I think

391
00:11:57.320 --> 00:11:59.620
as the de facto operating system

392
00:11:59.620 --> 00:12:01.380
for a bunch of engineers

393
00:12:01.380 --> 00:12:02.760
is sandboxing agents.

394
00:12:03.760 --> 00:12:05.000
I am probably preaching

395
00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:05.840
to the choir in here

396
00:12:05.840 --> 00:12:06.840
when I say that they were,

397
00:12:07.020 --> 00:12:08.160
the agents have clearly been

398
00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:09.100
a big step up

399
00:12:09.100 --> 00:12:09.880
in the capability.

400
00:12:10.100 --> 00:12:11.900
Like that is the lived experience

401
00:12:11.900 --> 00:12:12.480
for lots of people

402
00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:13.580
I speak to in my job,

403
00:12:13.600 --> 00:12:14.820
but also my own experience.

404
00:12:14.880 --> 00:12:15.700
It went from,

405
00:12:15.980 --> 00:12:16.760
hey, there's this cool bot

406
00:12:16.760 --> 00:12:17.920
I can talk to in my browser

407
00:12:17.920 --> 00:12:18.800
to, okay,

408
00:12:18.800 --> 00:12:19.380
I actually have

409
00:12:19.380 --> 00:12:20.220
like a fleet of robots

410
00:12:20.220 --> 00:12:20.880
that can half do

411
00:12:21.620 --> 00:12:22.640
some of the boring stuff

412
00:12:22.640 --> 00:12:23.120
and actually some

413
00:12:23.120 --> 00:12:24.020
of the more interesting stuff

414
00:12:24.020 --> 00:12:24.520
for me.

415
00:12:25.520 --> 00:12:26.360
And I think it's difficult

416
00:12:26.360 --> 00:12:26.900
to imagine,

417
00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:28.840
I imagine lots of you

418
00:12:28.840 --> 00:12:29.280
in a position

419
00:12:29.280 --> 00:12:30.080
where it's kind of difficult

420
00:12:30.080 --> 00:12:31.340
to imagine going back

421
00:12:31.340 --> 00:12:32.380
to doing certain classes

422
00:12:32.380 --> 00:12:33.300
of work without an agent.

423
00:12:33.440 --> 00:12:34.060
Like you're sitting there going,

424
00:12:34.220 --> 00:12:35.600
why was I ever doing this myself?

425
00:12:37.120 --> 00:12:38.360
You're probably also

426
00:12:38.360 --> 00:12:39.640
reading the horror stories

427
00:12:39.640 --> 00:12:40.140
on Reddit,

428
00:12:41.140 --> 00:12:42.280
sort of laughing and going,

429
00:12:42.360 --> 00:12:43.460
ha ha, that user did something silly.

430
00:12:43.540 --> 00:12:44.360
It deleted their home directory

431
00:12:44.360 --> 00:12:45.260
because they're idiots.

432
00:12:45.440 --> 00:12:46.020
And also in the back

433
00:12:46.020 --> 00:12:46.640
of your mind going,

434
00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:47.540
what if it deletes

435
00:12:47.540 --> 00:12:48.240
my home directory?

436
00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:50.100
I have personally not had

437
00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:51.020
any of those big fails,

438
00:12:51.040 --> 00:12:51.900
but I did have a fun one

439
00:12:51.900 --> 00:12:53.880
recently where a set

440
00:12:53.880 --> 00:12:54.840
of parallel agents

441
00:12:54.840 --> 00:12:56.440
were set off by Claude,

442
00:12:57.480 --> 00:12:58.460
which as part

443
00:12:58.460 --> 00:12:59.020
of a build process

444
00:12:59.020 --> 00:12:59.600
or something else,

445
00:12:59.680 --> 00:13:01.240
decided to build five copies

446
00:13:01.240 --> 00:13:02.320
of Node.js from source,

447
00:13:02.400 --> 00:13:03.480
completely exhausted the memory

448
00:13:03.480 --> 00:13:04.240
on my cloud server,

449
00:13:04.420 --> 00:13:04.860
which resulted

450
00:13:04.860 --> 00:13:06.000
in Tailsgale being um-killed

451
00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:06.820
and me no longer being able

452
00:13:06.820 --> 00:13:07.340
to talk to it.

453
00:13:08.380 --> 00:13:09.720
Not catastrophic data loss,

454
00:13:09.780 --> 00:13:10.540
but kind of annoying.

455
00:13:11.080 --> 00:13:13.260
And so lots of the agents

456
00:13:13.260 --> 00:13:14.140
are responding to this

457
00:13:14.140 --> 00:13:15.340
by telling you

458
00:13:15.340 --> 00:13:16.920
you can run slash sandbox

459
00:13:16.920 --> 00:13:17.940
and it's going to be fine.

460
00:13:19.680 --> 00:13:20.640
It might be fine.

461
00:13:21.080 --> 00:13:21.840
Like your confinement

462
00:13:22.560 --> 00:13:23.580
is a tough topic.

463
00:13:23.760 --> 00:13:24.220
It is something

464
00:13:24.220 --> 00:13:25.020
that we have been doing

465
00:13:25.020 --> 00:13:25.700
for years

466
00:13:25.700 --> 00:13:26.580
with our snap packages,

467
00:13:26.880 --> 00:13:27.920
for years with AppArmor,

468
00:13:28.140 --> 00:13:29.240
for years with virtual machines

469
00:13:29.240 --> 00:13:30.320
and micro VMs,

470
00:13:30.920 --> 00:13:32.900
and chucking a big honking

471
00:13:32.900 --> 00:13:33.540
Node.js code

472
00:13:33.540 --> 00:13:34.400
based on someone's machine

473
00:13:34.400 --> 00:13:34.860
and wrapping it

474
00:13:34.860 --> 00:13:35.400
with bubble wrap

475
00:13:35.400 --> 00:13:36.380
with a thousand exceptions,

476
00:13:37.020 --> 00:13:37.860
not that handy.

477
00:13:38.180 --> 00:13:40.700
It prevents some failures,

478
00:13:41.200 --> 00:13:41.580
and that's why

479
00:13:41.580 --> 00:13:42.260
the vast majority

480
00:13:42.260 --> 00:13:43.100
of you probably haven't

481
00:13:43.100 --> 00:13:43.920
had an epic fail

482
00:13:43.920 --> 00:13:45.540
and lived the YOLO life,

483
00:13:45.660 --> 00:13:46.380
dangerously skipped

484
00:13:46.380 --> 00:13:47.160
whatever it is,

485
00:13:47.980 --> 00:13:49.260
and are mostly okay.

486
00:13:49.640 --> 00:13:50.420
But I'm sure

487
00:13:50.420 --> 00:13:51.000
you've all experienced

488
00:13:51.000 --> 00:13:51.500
doing something

489
00:13:51.500 --> 00:13:52.840
you'd rather not have done,

490
00:13:52.840 --> 00:13:54.200
but you're all sensible

491
00:13:54.200 --> 00:13:54.740
and responsible,

492
00:13:54.920 --> 00:13:55.920
so recover from it just fine.

493
00:13:56.040 --> 00:13:56.540
No tears.

494
00:13:57.340 --> 00:13:58.160
The good news is

495
00:13:58.160 --> 00:13:59.320
we have a bunch of stuff

496
00:13:59.320 --> 00:14:00.120
that is out of the box

497
00:14:00.120 --> 00:14:00.540
on pretty much

498
00:14:00.540 --> 00:14:01.180
every Ubuntu machine

499
00:14:01.180 --> 00:14:01.540
on the planet

500
00:14:01.540 --> 00:14:02.420
that just makes this

501
00:14:02.420 --> 00:14:03.360
pretty simple.

502
00:14:03.620 --> 00:14:04.080
We actually have

503
00:14:04.080 --> 00:14:04.940
a really cool product

504
00:14:04.940 --> 00:14:06.040
which is one of the only things

505
00:14:06.040 --> 00:14:07.000
I have worked on in five years

506
00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:08.300
that is not yet open source.

507
00:14:08.600 --> 00:14:09.340
We will announce in April

508
00:14:09.340 --> 00:14:10.460
which I think is going to

509
00:14:10.460 --> 00:14:11.540
blow the doors off this thing.

510
00:14:11.920 --> 00:14:12.720
But it is built on top

511
00:14:12.720 --> 00:14:13.160
of all the things

512
00:14:13.160 --> 00:14:14.020
I'm going to talk about today.

513
00:14:14.420 --> 00:14:15.740
So one of which is LexD.

514
00:14:16.200 --> 00:14:17.280
This is a product

515
00:14:17.280 --> 00:14:18.740
that until I worked at Canonical

516
00:14:18.740 --> 00:14:19.880
I had no idea about,

517
00:14:20.000 --> 00:14:20.620
and about six months

518
00:14:20.620 --> 00:14:21.500
after I worked at Canonical

519
00:14:21.500 --> 00:14:22.220
I couldn't work out

520
00:14:22.220 --> 00:14:23.540
why nobody was talking about it.

521
00:14:23.920 --> 00:14:25.240
It is in the category

522
00:14:25.240 --> 00:14:25.700
of something

523
00:14:25.700 --> 00:14:26.420
that should be boring

524
00:14:26.420 --> 00:14:27.400
but I think is very cool.

525
00:14:27.700 --> 00:14:31.660
LexD is a clustered version of LexC

526
00:14:31.660 --> 00:14:33.320
which is a decade old

527
00:14:33.320 --> 00:14:34.840
piece of containerisation technology

528
00:14:34.840 --> 00:14:35.520
in the Linux kernel.

529
00:14:35.840 --> 00:14:36.620
It lets you get

530
00:14:36.620 --> 00:14:38.120
Linux system containers

531
00:14:38.120 --> 00:14:39.480
which are a bit like

532
00:14:39.480 --> 00:14:40.080
a Docker container

533
00:14:40.080 --> 00:14:41.340
but feel a bit more like a VM.

534
00:14:41.800 --> 00:14:43.440
So it's a container

535
00:14:43.440 --> 00:14:45.140
but it also runs systemd.

536
00:14:45.640 --> 00:14:46.920
And so it's much,

537
00:14:47.120 --> 00:14:48.180
it's kind of heavier weight

538
00:14:48.180 --> 00:14:49.320
than a Docker container

539
00:14:49.320 --> 00:14:50.200
but it still doesn't have

540
00:14:50.200 --> 00:14:50.700
its own kernel.

541
00:14:52.360 --> 00:14:53.780
And it also does virtual machines.

542
00:14:54.020 --> 00:14:54.400
And the API

543
00:14:54.400 --> 00:14:55.500
is basically identical

544
00:14:55.500 --> 00:14:56.680
like LexC launch

545
00:14:56.680 --> 00:14:57.860
and then you can LexC launch

546
00:14:57.860 --> 00:14:58.480
dash dash VM

547
00:14:58.480 --> 00:14:59.420
but all of the other

548
00:14:59.420 --> 00:15:00.500
dash dashes and configures

549
00:15:00.500 --> 00:15:01.260
exactly the same.

550
00:15:01.660 --> 00:15:02.700
But if you want a situation

551
00:15:02.700 --> 00:15:04.560
where actually you'd prefer

552
00:15:04.560 --> 00:15:06.500
a kernel, another kernel

553
00:15:06.500 --> 00:15:07.560
in between you and the agent

554
00:15:07.560 --> 00:15:08.720
for a little bit more separation

555
00:15:08.720 --> 00:15:09.520
you can do that.

556
00:15:10.040 --> 00:15:10.620
Personally I switch

557
00:15:10.620 --> 00:15:11.140
between the two

558
00:15:11.140 --> 00:15:12.260
depending on what project

559
00:15:12.260 --> 00:15:12.920
I'm working on

560
00:15:12.920 --> 00:15:13.880
and the source code I'm writing.

561
00:15:14.360 --> 00:15:16.000
Sometimes I launch a VM,

562
00:15:16.140 --> 00:15:17.040
sometimes I launch a container.

563
00:15:17.240 --> 00:15:18.040
The script is identical

564
00:15:18.040 --> 00:15:19.180
apart from one flag.

565
00:15:19.180 --> 00:15:21.260
And this is broadly

566
00:15:21.260 --> 00:15:22.140
